      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "1. First, we need to determine the slope of the line $2x + 3y = 6$. To do this, we can rearrange the given equation into the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope.\n\n2. Starting with the given equation $2x + 3y = 6$, we'll isolate $y$ on one side of the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   3y = -2x + 6\n   \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides by $3$ to get $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n   \\]\n\n4. Now we can see that the slope $m$ of the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\n5. The line we're looking for is perpendicular to the line with slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$. The slopes of two perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals of each other. So, if $m_1$ is the slope of the first line, then the slope of the line perpendicular to it ($m_2$) is given by $m_2 = -\\frac{1}{m_1}$.\n\n6. Therefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to $2x + 3y = 6$ is:\n\n   \\[\n   m_2 = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n\nConclusion: The slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
